 25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{5\\times 5}_{⏟}}_{25}\\times 5 & =25\\times 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out ${\\color{blue}25} \\times 5.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{2}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5^3=125.$" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138137 1071 "Find the value of $6^1$." 13194139534624 17592186045417 false] [17592186138137 1071 "Find the value of $6^1.$" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138137 1074 "Here, ${\\color{blue}6}^{\\color{red}1}$ is in exponent form. The base is $\\color{blue}6$ and the exponent is ${\\color{red}1}$.\n\nThis means that $\\color{blue}6$ is taken as a factor only once. Therefore,\n$6^1 = 6$." 13194139534624 17592186045417 false] [17592186138137 1074 "Here, ${\\color{blue}6}^{\\color{red}1}$ is in exponent form. The base is $\\color{blue}6$ and the exponent is ${\\color{red}1}.$ \n\nThis means that $\\color{blue}6$ is taken as a factor only once. Therefore,\n\n$$\n6^1 = 6.\n$$" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138138 1071 "Calculate the value of $0^5$." 13194139534624 17592186045417 false] [17592186138138 1071 "Calculate the value of $0^5.$" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138138 1074 "Here, ${\\color{blue}0}^{\\color{red}5}$ is in exponent form. The base is $\\color{blue}0$ and the exponent is ${\\color{red}5}$.\n\nThis means that ${\\color{blue}0}$ is taken as a factor ${\\color{red}5}$ times. Therefore,\n$5$" 13194139534624 17592186045417 false] [17592186138138 1074 "Here, ${\\color{blue}0}^{\\color{red}5}$ is in exponent form. The base is $\\color{blue}0$ and the exponent is ${\\color{red}5}.$ \n\nThis means that ${\\color{blue}0}$ is taken as a factor ${\\color{red}5}$ times. Therefore,\n![](images/ac/acfc8529f86ae7a3d9082c541da994c0c69ffccf33d01226593ae61cfb4d263a.svg)" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138401 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429896 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138401 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429899 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138401 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429901 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138401 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429902 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138401 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429907 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138402 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429892 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138402 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429897 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138402 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429904 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138402 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429906 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138402 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429908 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138403 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429891 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138403 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429893 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138403 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429895 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138403 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429898 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138403 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429900 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429890 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429894 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429903 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186429905 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138624 1050 420.0 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138714 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138714 1071 "$0^{4} =$ {{field-1}}" 13194139534624 17592186045417 false] [17592186138714 1071 "${0}^{4}=${{field-1}}" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186138714 1074 "Here, ${0}^{4}$ is in exponent form. The base is $0$ and the exponent is $4.$\n\nThis means that $0$ is taken as a factor $4$ times. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{0}^{4}={{0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ times}}=0.\n$$" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186139702 1064 "To evaluate a power, we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the resulting product.\n\nSo, to find the value of $9^2$, we first write it out with multiplication signs as follows:\n$2$\n\nThen, we compute the product:\n\n$$\n9^{2} = 9 \\cdot 9 = 81\n$$\n\nRaising a number to the power of $2$ is known as **squaring** a number. Therefore, the following are equivalent.\n\n- $9^2 = 81$\n- The **square** of $9$ is $81$.\n- $9$ raised to the *second* power equals $81$." 13194139534624 17592186045417 false] [17592186139702 1064 "To evaluate a power, we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the resulting product.\n\nSo, to find the value of $9^2,$ we first write it out with multiplication signs as follows:\n![](images/c7/c7e4007e9b80b07aca028ffc9bf8f5c4354bafb4e15dd0ce7e474279e364e1a3.svg)\n\nThen, we compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2}=9\\times 9=81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRaising a number to the power of $2$ is known as **squaring** a number. Therefore, the following are equivalent.\n\n- $9^2 = 81$\n\n- The **square** of $9$ is $81.$\n\n- $9$ raised to the *second* power equals $81.$" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186139743 1064 "Let's evaluate the following exponent:\n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing our exponent as repeated multiplication:\n![](images/fe/fea572ac021fd3eabd4d5b1ade6692cbb1507d993b16616f63b8aa78259d3ade.svg)\n\nNow, we compute the product. \n\nSince $2 \\times 2 = 4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{2\\times 2}_{⏟}}_{4}\\times 2 & =4\\times 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${\\color{blue}{4}}\\times 2=8,$ we have $2^3=8.$\n\nRaising a number to the power of $3$ is known as **cubing** a number. Therefore, the following are equivalent.\n\n- $2^3 = 8$\n\n- The **cube** of $2$ is $8.$\n\n- $2$ raised to the *third* power equals $8.$" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186139743 1064 "Let's evaluate the following exponent:\n\n$$\n2^{\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing our exponent as repeated multiplication:\n$3$\n\nNow, we compute the product.\n\nSince\n\n$$\n2 \\times 2 = 4\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 \\cdot 2_(⏟)_(4) \\cdot 2 &= 4 \\cdot 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{4}}\\times 2=8\n$$\n\nwe have $2^3=8$.\n\nRaising a number to the power of $3$ is known as **cubing** a number. Therefore, the following are equivalent.\n\n- $2^3 = 8$\n- The **cube** of $2$ is $8$.\n- $2$ raised to the *third* power equals $8$." 13194139534624 17592186045417 false] [17592186429819 1080 "9" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186429819 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186429819 1083 #uuid "00542389-ab01-5496-b4cb-a8421904c56d" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186429820 1080 "25" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186429820 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186429820 1083 #uuid "07edbe8b-8b99-5551-8907-2d61816753eb" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186429821 1080 "130" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186429821 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186429821 1083 #uuid "08e7a713-c709-5a51-b43d-cd9dc53ed07c" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186429822 1080 "343" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186429822 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186429822 1083 #uuid "0c0f287a-82ae-555f-a79b-907b97a0e817" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186429823 1080 "1" 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186429823 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534624 17592186045417 true] [17592186429823 1083 #uuid "0fea5572-6354-53eb